    "updated_at": "2026-07-28T15:14:25Z",
    "body": "> 1. Is `symmetrize_spin` meant to improve the spin character of the returned state, or only to make the symmetric sector representable? The docstring reads like the former to me and the measurement matches the latter. If it's the latter, I'm happy to send a docs PR saying so, with these numbers in it.\n> 2. Given [Allow passing **kwargs in `diagonalize_fermionic_hamiltonian` function #248](https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-addon-sqd/issues/248), is `functools.partial(solve_sci_batch, spin_sq=...)` simply the intended way to target a spin sector from the top-level driver? A one-line \"yes, that's the pattern\" is a complete answer and I'll document it.\n> \n\n`symmetrize_spin` is a flag that specifies whether you want augment your sampled subspace by creating new configurations by using alpha CI strs as beta strs projection diagonalization, and vice versa beta CI strs may be used as alpha strs during projection.\n\n> On [#203](https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-addon-sqd/issues/203): `open_shell` is still on `solve_fermion`, `optimize_orbitals` and `bitstring_matrix_to_ci_strs` (`fermion.py:543`, `:647`, `:795`) while the newer driver has moved to `symmetrize_spin`. Rather than add another opinion about the naming, treat the above as measurements of what the current default actually produces on a published benchmark.\n\nI'm not sure what your question is here. Please use the bug report issue template to report a specific bug with the software.",
