    "body": "Thanks. That settles 1: the flag augments the sampled subspace by cross-using alpha strings as beta strings and back. That is which determinants span the space, not what spin the returned state comes out in, which is what the measurements show.\n\nOne correction to my own wording: fermion.py:216 is already accurate about the mechanism. What read as spin improvement to me was the parameter name plus the phrase \"spin symmetrization\" in the argument checks (:260, :272), with nothing saying the flag leaves the spin of the result alone. So the docs change I had in mind is additive, roughly:\n\nThis affects only which determinants span the diagonalization subspace. It does not impose or improve the total spin of the returned state. To target a spin sector, pass a solver with spin_sq set; SCIState.spin_square() reports the value obtained.\n\nI'll send it as a PR rather than keep asking here, so it's easy to reject in review.\n\nQuestion 2 is still open and is a yes or no. With sci_solver=None the batch solver is called positionally at :370, so spin_sq is never set on the default path. Is functools.partial(solve_sci_batch, spin_sq=0.0) the intended way to target a spin sector from diagonalize_fermionic_hamiltonian? If yes, I'll document that in the same PR.\n\nOn #203: no question and no bug there, it was context for why I asked about the flag rather than proposing another rename. Withdrawing it.",
